## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design runtime-extensible behavior via a registry and strategy-like eviction policies, with testable components and clear extension points.

## Key Concepts

- Strategy pattern
- Registry for dynamic policy binding
- Runtime extensibility without core changes
- Testable policy interactions

## Code Example

```typescript
type Entry = { id: string; lastAccess: number; size: number };

interface IPolicy {
  onAccess(entry: Entry): void;
  onEvict(entries: Entry[]): boolean;
}

class LRUPolicy implements IPolicy {
  onAccess(_e: Entry) { /* update recency */ }
  onEvict(_entries: Entry[]): boolean { /* evict oldest */ return true; }
}

class TTLPolicy implements IPolicy {
  constructor(private ttl: number) {}
  onAccess(_e: Entry) { /* refresh */ }
  onEvict(_entries: Entry[]): boolean { /* compare now - lastAccess > ttl */ return true; }
}

class PolicyRegistry {
  private map = new Map<string, IPolicy>();
  register(name: string, p: IPolicy) { this.map.set(name, p); }
  resolve(name: string): IPolicy { return this.map.get(name)!; }
}
```

